1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,392
Sea of Cortez. Midnight.

2
00:00:02,892 --> 00:00:07,184
You're twelve metres down, and your torch
is the only light in the water.

3
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:09,425
Then the beam catches something.

4
00:00:09,925 --> 00:00:10,786
One shape.

5
00:00:11,136 --> 00:00:11,987
Then ten.

6
00:00:12,337 --> 00:00:13,732
Then too many to count.

7
00:00:14,332 --> 00:00:17,160
And every single one of them... is turning
red.

8
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,403
The fishermen here have a name for them.

9
00:00:20,703 --> 00:00:21,943
Diablo rojo.

10
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:23,305
The red devil.

11
00:00:24,505 --> 00:00:29,548
Now, when you picture a dangerous animal
in the ocean, you probably picture a

12
00:00:29,548 --> 00:00:29,941
shark.

13
00:00:30,241 --> 00:00:32,593
One big predator, one set of teeth.

14
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:34,133
This is different.

15
00:00:34,532 --> 00:00:39,564
A Humboldt squid isn't a giant. The
biggest are about the length of an adult

16
00:00:39,564 --> 00:00:39,961
human.

17
00:00:40,361 --> 00:00:42,543
But it almost never shows up alone.

18
00:00:42,843 --> 00:00:46,691
It hunts in the dark, in groups that can
number in the hundreds.

19
00:00:46,991 --> 00:00:51,593
And when it gets excited, its whole body
starts flashing like a warning light.

20
00:00:52,193 --> 00:00:53,820
So why do they turn red?

21
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,990
Hold that thought. First, let's open the
file.

22
00:00:57,790 --> 00:01:01,082
Encounter File number one. Dosidicus
gigas.

23
00:01:01,382 --> 00:01:04,794
The Humboldt squid, sometimes called the
jumbo squid.

24
00:01:05,294 --> 00:01:10,568
It lives in the eastern Pacific, from
Chile all the way up to Mexico, and

25
00:01:10,568 --> 00:01:12,519
lately, much further north.

26
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:18,115
A big one can have a body about one and a
half metres long and weigh around fifty

27
00:01:18,115 --> 00:01:18,756
kilograms.

28
00:01:19,056 --> 00:01:23,959
That's roughly the weight of a large dog,
packed into a torpedo of pure muscle.

29
00:01:24,559 --> 00:01:26,657
During the day, it's nowhere near you.

30
00:01:26,957 --> 00:01:32,614
It sits hundreds of metres down, in water
so low in oxygen that most fish can't

31
00:01:32,614 --> 00:01:34,261
survive there for long.

32
00:01:34,561 --> 00:01:37,767
The squid can. It slows itself down and
waits.

33
00:01:38,367 --> 00:01:41,860
Then the sun sets. And the whole
population rises.

34
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:47,037
Every night, they travel up toward the
surface to feed, and by midnight they can

35
00:01:47,037 --> 00:01:49,354
be right where a night diver would be.

36
00:01:49,654 --> 00:01:50,933
Like, say... you.

37
00:01:51,833 --> 00:01:55,729
So what happens when they get there? Let's
go through the equipment.

38
00:01:56,229 --> 00:01:57,286
First, speed.

39
00:01:57,586 --> 00:02:00,294
A Humboldt squid swims by jet propulsion.

40
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:05,297
It sucks water into its body and blasts it
out through a tube called a siphon.

41
00:02:05,597 --> 00:02:09,560
That can push it to over twenty kilometres
an hour in short bursts.

42
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:13,479
A good swimmer in fins manages maybe three
or four.

43
00:02:13,779 --> 00:02:15,346
You are not winning a race here.

44
00:02:15,946 --> 00:02:17,122
Second, the arms.

45
00:02:17,422 --> 00:02:21,332
Eight arms and two longer tentacles, all
covered in suckers.

46
00:02:21,632 --> 00:02:25,924
And these aren't the soft, rubbery suckers
you'd find on an octopus.

47
00:02:26,124 --> 00:02:28,513
Each one is ringed with tiny, sharp teeth.

48
00:02:28,813 --> 00:02:32,624
Once they close on something, they don't
slide off. They dig in.

49
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:34,315
Third, the beak.

50
00:02:34,615 --> 00:02:39,319
Right in the middle of all those arms sits
a hard, hooked beak, a lot like a

51
00:02:39,319 --> 00:02:39,876
parrot's.

52
00:02:40,176 --> 00:02:43,616
It's built to tear flesh into pieces small
enough to swallow.

53
00:02:44,216 --> 00:02:46,081
So: speed, grip, and a blade.

54
00:02:46,381 --> 00:02:48,595
Now multiply it by a few hundred.

55
00:02:49,195 --> 00:02:52,271
These squid don't just happen to be in the
same place.

56
00:02:52,471 --> 00:02:56,750
Sonar studies suggest they may actually
coordinate when they hunt at night.

57
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,078
Which brings us back to that question.

58
00:02:59,978 --> 00:03:01,299
Why do they turn red?

59
00:03:01,799 --> 00:03:05,281
To find out, scientists did something
slightly unhinged.

60
00:03:05,581 --> 00:03:10,690
They strapped cameras onto live Humboldt
squid and released them back into the sea.

61
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:12,011
A squid-cam.

62
00:03:12,611 --> 00:03:13,978
What they saw was this.

63
00:03:14,278 --> 00:03:19,274
The squid's skin is packed with tiny
colour cells, and in this species, they

64
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:21,180
come in just one colour: red.

65
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:23,980
Expand them, and the squid goes deep red.

66
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:26,729
Squeeze them shut, and it turns pale
white.

67
00:03:26,929 --> 00:03:30,325
And it can switch between the two several
times a second.

68
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:32,571
Here's the interesting part.

69
00:03:32,871 --> 00:03:36,693
The flashing almost always happened when
other squid were in view.

70
00:03:36,993 --> 00:03:41,724
The researchers think it's a signal, a way
for squid to talk to each other in the

71
00:03:41,724 --> 00:03:42,016
dark.

72
00:03:42,316 --> 00:03:46,031
They can even change the speed and the
strength of the flashing.

73
00:03:46,631 --> 00:03:51,509
So when the water around you turns into a
field of flashing red, you're not looking

74
00:03:51,509 --> 00:03:52,273
at a warning.

75
00:03:52,573 --> 00:03:54,413
You're looking at a conversation.

76
00:03:54,813 --> 00:03:57,811
And you have absolutely no idea what's
being said.

77
00:03:58,111 --> 00:03:59,527
Which is somehow worse.

78
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:03,246
Okay. So they're fast, they grab, and they
talk.

79
00:04:03,546 --> 00:04:06,657
But has any of this actually been used
against people?

80
00:04:07,157 --> 00:04:11,967
Here's where we need to be careful,
because this animal attracts a lot of tall

81
00:04:11,967 --> 00:04:12,337
tales.

82
00:04:12,937 --> 00:04:14,455
Let's start with the legend.

83
00:04:14,755 --> 00:04:20,346
Fishermen in the Sea of Cortez have long
told stories of men who fell overboard at

84
00:04:20,346 --> 00:04:24,301
night and were never found, and they blame
the red devils.

85
00:04:24,601 --> 00:04:27,330
Those stories are old and widely repeated.

86
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:32,051
But we found no confirmed case of a
Humboldt squid killing a human.

87
00:04:32,351 --> 00:04:34,730
So that one goes in the legend pile.

88
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:36,853
Now, the real accounts.

89
00:04:37,353 --> 00:04:43,239
Scott Cassell is a diver and filmmaker who
has spent years diving with Humboldt squid

90
00:04:43,239 --> 00:04:43,931
in Mexico.

91
00:04:44,231 --> 00:04:47,387
And by his own account, it hasn't always
gone well.

92
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:52,558
He has said a squid once dragged him down
so fast that his eardrum ruptured.

93
00:04:52,858 --> 00:04:55,809
He's described having his arm pulled out
of its socket.

94
00:04:56,109 --> 00:04:58,990
And another squid, he says, tore into his
scalp.

95
00:04:59,290 --> 00:05:03,345
Today, he dives with them wearing a custom
suit of body armour.

96
00:05:04,045 --> 00:05:05,804
And it's not just one diver.

97
00:05:06,104 --> 00:05:12,633
In the summer of 2009, thousands of jumbo
squid suddenly showed up in shallow water

98
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:15,858
off San Diego, near La Jolla, California.

99
00:05:16,158 --> 00:05:20,731
Divers reported squid grabbing at their
masks, their cameras, and their gear.

100
00:05:20,931 --> 00:05:24,218
And some decided to stay out of the water
altogether.

101
00:05:24,818 --> 00:05:27,535
But listen to how the scientists described
it.

102
00:05:27,835 --> 00:05:32,849
One biologist pointed out that these squid
aren't the man-eaters they'd been made out

103
00:05:32,849 --> 00:05:33,203
to be.

104
00:05:33,503 --> 00:05:36,969
A lot of the grabbing looked more like
curiosity than attack.

105
00:05:37,569 --> 00:05:41,487
Picture an animal that explores the world
with arms full of teeth.

106
00:05:41,787 --> 00:05:43,709
Curiosity still leaves marks.

107
00:05:44,709 --> 00:05:46,518
So let's put you back in the water.

108
00:05:46,818 --> 00:05:50,701
Midnight. Twelve metres down. Red flashes
all around you.

109
00:05:51,001 --> 00:05:51,800
Your move.

110
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,765
Option one. Swim for the surface.

111
00:05:55,065 --> 00:05:58,108
The problem: they're several times faster
than you.

112
00:05:58,408 --> 00:06:03,498
And shooting straight up from depth is one
of the most dangerous things a diver can

113
00:06:03,498 --> 00:06:03,682
do.

114
00:06:03,882 --> 00:06:06,754
A rapid ascent can seriously injure your
lungs.

115
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:10,400
You'd be escaping one danger by swimming
into another.

116
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,347
Option two. Stay perfectly still.

117
00:06:13,647 --> 00:06:14,711
Honestly, better.

118
00:06:15,011 --> 00:06:19,040
But remember, a Humboldt squid
investigates with its arms.

119
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:23,410
Staying still might just make you a more
interesting thing to touch.

120
00:06:24,010 --> 00:06:26,320
Option three. Turn off your torch.

121
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:27,831
This one's tempting.

122
00:06:28,131 --> 00:06:33,073
And here's a strange fact: squid boats in
this part of the world use bright lights

123
00:06:33,073 --> 00:06:34,640
to draw squid in at night.

124
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:36,882
Light really does bring them closer.

125
00:06:37,182 --> 00:06:42,503
But switch it off, and now you're blind,
in the dark, surrounded by animals with

126
00:06:42,503 --> 00:06:44,698
huge eyes built for exactly this.

127
00:06:44,998 --> 00:06:47,046
They can see you. You can't see them.

128
00:06:47,646 --> 00:06:49,199
Option four. Fight back.

129
00:06:49,499 --> 00:06:53,592
With what? Ten arms, hundreds of teeth,
and a few hundred friends.

130
00:06:54,292 --> 00:06:57,260
So what do people who dive with them
actually do?

131
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:58,644
They plan for it.

132
00:06:58,944 --> 00:07:04,414
They dive with protection, stay calm, stay
together, and avoid the frenzy around

133
00:07:04,414 --> 00:07:08,790
injured or hooked squid, because that's
when things get chaotic.

134
00:07:09,090 --> 00:07:14,289
In other words, the real answer is to
never end up alone in that water without a

135
00:07:14,289 --> 00:07:14,614
plan.

136
00:07:15,614 --> 00:07:20,316
And speaking of injured squid, here's
something that might change how you see

137
00:07:20,316 --> 00:07:20,866
all this.

138
00:07:21,266 --> 00:07:23,068
Humboldt squid are cannibals.

139
00:07:23,368 --> 00:07:27,834
When one gets hurt, say, caught on a
fishing line, the others will often turn

140
00:07:27,834 --> 00:07:28,182
on it.

141
00:07:28,482 --> 00:07:31,280
Your fellow red devil is also on the menu.

142
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:33,353
They also live fast.

143
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:38,091
Most Humboldt squid only live around a
year. Two, if they're lucky.

144
00:07:38,391 --> 00:07:43,257
Everything you've seen, the speed, the
hunting, the flashing, happens in one

145
00:07:43,257 --> 00:07:44,538
short, intense life.

146
00:07:45,138 --> 00:07:49,615
And they're doing something few animals
manage right now. They're expanding.

147
00:07:49,915 --> 00:07:55,469
Over the past couple of decades, they've
pushed north from Mexico along the coast,

148
00:07:55,469 --> 00:07:56,959
all the way to Alaska.

149
00:07:57,259 --> 00:08:02,498
One leading idea is that low-oxygen zones
in the ocean are spreading, and that's

150
00:08:02,498 --> 00:08:07,868
exactly the kind of water this squid can
handle and many of its competitors can't.

151
00:08:08,168 --> 00:08:11,908
The ocean is changing, and this animal
seems to be winning.

152
00:08:12,608 --> 00:08:13,865
But here's the twist.

153
00:08:14,265 --> 00:08:18,938
For all the stories about the red devil
attacking people, the numbers go

154
00:08:18,938 --> 00:08:20,820
overwhelmingly the other way.

155
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:25,413
Humboldt squid support one of the biggest
squid fisheries on the planet.

156
00:08:25,713 --> 00:08:30,664
In 2014, Peru alone landed more than half
a million tonnes of them.

157
00:08:30,964 --> 00:08:36,525
Even if every single squid were a fifty-
kilo giant, that's more than ten million

158
00:08:36,525 --> 00:08:39,134
animals in one year, from one country.

159
00:08:39,734 --> 00:08:44,618
If you've ever eaten a squid steak or a
plate of calamari, there's a real chance

160
00:08:44,618 --> 00:08:46,144
it came from this animal.

161
00:08:46,744 --> 00:08:48,672
So... who's really the devil here?

162
00:08:49,872 --> 00:08:53,004
Back to the water. Midnight. Twelve metres
down.

163
00:08:53,304 --> 00:08:57,532
The flashing slowly fades, as the shapes
slip back into the dark.

164
00:08:58,132 --> 00:09:03,116
Somewhere below you right now, a few
hundred metres down, thousands of them are

165
00:09:03,116 --> 00:09:05,451
flashing red and white at each other.

166
00:09:05,751 --> 00:09:08,174
We still don't know exactly what they're
saying.

167
00:09:08,674 --> 00:09:12,258
But if you ever see the water around you
turn red, remember.

168
00:09:12,558 --> 00:09:14,285
It probably isn't about you.

169
00:09:14,986 --> 00:09:15,786
Probably.

170
00:09:16,986 --> 00:09:18,974
That was Encounter File number one.

171
00:09:19,274 --> 00:09:24,422
Which animal should we open next? Tell me
in the comments. I read every single one.

